# SCM syntax highlighting & preventing 3-way merges
pixi.lock merge=binary linguist-language=YAML linguist-generated=true -diff

# gofmt is byte-strict about LF-only line endings (M21 Phase 5, #242 — learned on the since-deleted
# Go sidecar); kept generic so any future Go file checks out LF on Windows.
*.go text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and patch files must stay LF on every platform. A CRLF shell script fails on
# Linux with `bash: \r: command not found` on its first line, and `git apply` rejects a CRLF
# patch against LF sources -- both would break CI's Linux jobs while passing on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.patch -text

# Git hooks are shell scripts with no extension, so `*.sh` above does not reach them. Same failure,
# one step earlier: a CRLF `#!/bin/sh` is not a valid shebang, so the hook would not merely misbehave
# on a Linux clone -- it would fail to execute at all, and a hook that cannot run is a gate that
# silently is not there. Git warned about exactly this conversion when .githooks/pre-push was added.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The committed vendor-survey index (#589). Generated on whichever machine ran the survey and
# read by `audit-completeness` in CI on Linux -- pin LF so the file does not churn per platform.
.vendor-survey/ledger.tsv text eol=lf
